"""Generate reproducible cue/second grids from retrieved captions plus manual coding.
The codes are editorial interpretations, not measured audience reactions.
"""
import csv,json,math
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path.cwd();OUT=ROOT/'docs/research/extinct-script-forensics';OUT.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
DATA=json.loads((ROOT/'docs/research/data/extinct-script-study/retrieval.json').read_text())
# cue-start, cue-end inclusive, function, observed mechanism, useful adaptation, risk
DNA=[
(0,5,'Embodied cold open','A small organism encounters a physical trap before the subject becomes a laboratory explanation. Place, material and irreversible action carry the scene.','Open on a surprising identifiable trace in dirt, not an introduction to the channel or formation name.','Do not invent a witnessed mastodon death or an exact ancient afternoon.'),
(6,8,'Evidence claim enters','The amber story jumps to researchers sampling the trapped insect. A concrete object survives the time jump, keeping the narrative thread readable.','Keep one fragment identifiable from environmental shedding through mineral contact and the lab.','A reconstruction needs a nearby qualifier; a rendered object is not recovered footage.'),
(9,12,'First reversal','The apparently extraordinary DNA result is placed under doubt. The opening claim becomes a question rather than a completed boast.','Reveal the mastodon signal early, then ask how an old fragment survives and how a false match is rejected.','A reveal must not be deliberately misstated just to create a later correction.'),
(13,18,'Bounded promise','The script offers a qualified answer: ancient DNA is possible, but not for every fossil or age. A limit becomes part of the promise.','Promise a readable fragment of an ecosystem, not a whole genome or resurrection.','Do not turn a scientific boundary into an exhausting list of disclaimers before any reward.'),
(19,30,'Credible foothold','A younger, real specimen gives the audience a concrete success case and explains what genetic comparison can reveal.','Explain the extraction/reference comparison with one simple example, not a catalogue of lab jargon.','The older Quagga example is storytelling evidence here, not new evidence about Greenland.'),
(31,42,'Attractive hypothesis','Preserved tissues and amber make the earlier claim feel plausible before the failure is explained. Small exceptions interrupt a smooth but overconfident story.','Let cold and mineral binding look useful, then show why useful is not indestructible.','Do not borrow the historical amber assumptions as a modern preservation guarantee.'),
(43,48,'The problem gets personal','DNA damage is reframed as a process that even living bodies contend with; the subject briefly connects to the listener.','Use the idea that organisms leave material behind throughout life to make environmental DNA intuitive.','Do not claim the viewer can find two-million-year-old DNA in any random patch of soil.'),
(49,59,'Mechanism before terminology overload','Damage, water and cellular repair explain why death changes the preservation problem. The mechanism makes a technical term useful.','Say fragments break before introducing the mineral shelter; show contact before a chemistry label.','Do not turn simplified motion into a measured chemical simulation.'),
(60,66,'Question refresh','The original old-DNA puzzle is reactivated as a question about the timescale of readability.','After explaining environmental DNA, return to why any of it survived approximately two million years.','A question refresh should narrow the problem, not repeat an already answered teaser.'),
(67,78,'Quantified contrast','Different DNA-decay examples demonstrate that preservation context matters; the contrast carries the exposition.','Use cold and mineral conditions as interacting filters rather than one universal expiry date.','The 2018 numerical examples must not become a universal 2026 DNA lifetime.'),
(79,84,'Object state clarifies','Fragmentation is distinguished from simply disappearing, then preservation conditions are introduced.','Animate an intact short illustrative strand splitting, followed by separate fates for the pieces.','Do not imply every visible fragment corresponds to an actual sequenced molecule.'),
(85,93,'Limit and historical record','Best-case preservation and the contemporary oldest-DNA discussion constrain the initial promise.','Date the 2022 Greenland study explicitly; avoid a current-world-record claim.','The episode predates the Greenland result and includes historical limits that cannot be copied as current facts.'),
(94,98,'Capability payoff','An actual sequencing achievement pays back the earlier claim that fragments can still be useful.','Turn sequence matching into a visible partial identification, not a claim that the whole animal has returned.','Do not visually assemble a complete mastodon genome from the Greenland fragments.'),
(99,102,'Opening mystery revisited','The gap between readable-DNA limits and the much older insect claim forces a specific answer.','Ask what else could produce a convincing genetic signal before showing contamination checks.','Our story should not invent a fraudulent Greenland result; the question is methodological, not an accusation.'),
(103,110,'Necessary technical detour','Amplification is explained as the step needed to obtain enough material to read. The technical process has a clear story job.','Introduce only the extraction/read/reference steps needed to understand the identification problem.','Do not imply the Greenland protocol was exactly the same as the historical PCR example.'),
(111,130,'Causal reveal','Sensitivity, contamination and shared sequence material explain why the opening claim failed. The answer is a mechanism, not another superlative.','Show why newer contamination and relatives with similar sequences must be tested, without fake success percentages.','The historical insect reversal must not be transplanted onto the validated Greenland finding.'),
(131,140,'Trust repaired','The script answers the possible overreaction that no fossil DNA can be trusted, then gives contamination-control context.','Place the caveat next to the test that addresses it, so uncertainty adds understanding rather than cancelling discovery.','Avoid implying one checklist or a clean room proves every identification automatically.'),
(141,155,'Evidence earns wonder','Concrete research uses supply rewards after the method and failure discussion.','Reveal the richer plant/animal community after the viewer understands how a partial match is useful.','Do not borrow human/horse/bird findings as if they were recovered from this Greenland sample.'),
(156,165,'Model refinement','Later findings complicate a simplistic decay model instead of pretending the limit is permanently settled.','Preserve the distinction between an age estimate, survival conditions and taxonomic precision.','A popular script is not an authority for present-day scientific limits.'),
(166,180,'Callback with open future','The closing returns to genetic fragments and the opening specimen; future possibility keeps the topic open.','End on the same dirt and incomplete trace, with the animal/story identity as a callback.','Reject the tempting leap from future technology to recoverable non-avian dinosaur DNA; do not copy that speculative promise.')]
WHALES=[
(0,4,'Familiar model','The opening establishes the familiar modern animal and its apparent dependence on the sea.','Use a familiar Arctic expectation only long enough to set up the surprising Greenland trace.','Do not spend a minute establishing what the title already implies.'),
(5,6,'Concrete contradiction','A very short pivot replaces the familiar whale model with the claim that whales could walk.','Put the mastodon lineage in the first few lines, then state that the evidence came from dirt, not its skeleton.','Surprise must be accurate; mastodons are not mammoths and a signal is not a recovered body.'),
(7,13,'Transformation promise','A large biological transition is framed through physical scale and a sequence of fossil forms.','Promise the journey from one broken fragment to a partially reconstructed community.','Do not promise to recover every species or show exact appearance.'),
(14,17,'Named intermediate anchor','One memorable transitional animal carries the abstract evolutionary problem.','Use one identifiable fragment as a physical anchor instead of changing symbols at every edit.','A rendered fragment remains an illustrative representative, not an actual recovered read.'),
(18,22,'Apparent design failure','Awkward locomotion in two environments creates a problem the next section can answer.','Show why a short damaged sequence initially appears insufficient for an identification.','Do not anthropomorphise evolution as bad design or create a false laboratory crisis.'),
(23,29,'Contextual reframe','The environment explains why the animal was functionally suited despite the apparent disadvantages. An analogy supplies a fast mental model.','Reframe dirt from passive container to a chemically active preservation environment.','Keep the mineral mechanism qualified; some protection is not a guarantee.'),
(30,33,'Analogy boundary','The script separates a crocodile-like lifestyle from actual ancestry and introduces the relationship puzzle.','Separate our damaged-archive metaphor from literal genetic data; explain what the analogy cannot show.','Do not infer genetic relationship from a visual resemblance.'),
(34,38,'First evidence channel','Genetic comparison produces a surprising relationship; fossils then provide another line of support.','Sequence comparison should lead naturally to evidence convergence, not a dramatic unsupported species guess.','Public genetic comparisons are not our raw sample reads.'),
(39,46,'Tangible diagnostic clue','A specific anatomical feature makes the abstract relationship argument inspectable.','Give the audience a concrete reason a short DNA segment may match some relatives but not resolve a precise species.','Do not fabricate diagnostic bases or label decorative bars as actual data.'),
(47,53,'Correction plus second clue','A classification correction and ankle anatomy prevent the audience from flattening relatives into direct ancestors.','State mastodon lineage rather than silently selecting a familiar exact animal species.','Do not hide taxonomic uncertainty in the credits.'),
(54,58,'Synthesis','The relationship of the examples is explicitly reassembled for the viewer.','After method details, recap in one sentence what can now be inferred and what cannot.','A recap must add resolution rather than restart the introduction.'),
(59,61,'Transformation payoff','The marine endpoint closes the major physical-transition promise.','Let the evidence-supported community appear as an earned payoff, not as forest filler.','Illustration of an ecosystem is not an observed simultaneous scene.'),
(62,65,'Boundary becomes new question','The script distinguishes knowing the transition from knowing its cause.','Distinguish identifying contributors from replaying one ancient afternoon.','Do not claim uncertainty about exact behaviour makes all the evidence unreliable.'),
(66,72,'Hypotheses tested','Possible causes are offered alongside a counterexample, preventing one neat explanation from becoming false certainty.','Show transport, time accumulation, preservation and reference gaps as distinct filters.','Missing DNA is not proof that an organism was absent.'),
(73,76,'Owned uncertainty and scale','An unanswered question is admitted; a compressed scale analogy restores wonder without pretending to solve it.','Make incomplete knowledge part of the achievement rather than a lengthy apology.','A metaphor is not a measurement; avoid invented probabilities.'),
(77,78,'Embodied callback','The closing returns to the intermediary and the original familiar animal.','Return to the same sediment and surviving fragment; close the title promise with a changed perspective.','Do not insert a new mystery or long subscription speech after the emotional landing.')]
MANUAL={'eons-dna':DNA,'eons-whales':WHALES}
summary=[]
for case in DATA['cases']:
 if case['key'] not in MANUAL or case['status']!='timed_captions_retrieved':continue
 cues=case['cues'];beats=[];seen=[]
 for n,(a,b,role,mechanism,adapt,risk) in enumerate(MANUAL[case['key']],1):
  span=cues[a:b+1];seen.extend(range(a,b+1));beats.append({'beat':n,'first_cue':a,'last_cue':b,'start':span[0]['start'],'end':span[-1]['end'],'role':role,'observed_textual_mechanism':mechanism,'editorial_attention_hypothesis':'This may help a viewer understand the next question or payoff; retention is not observed.','adaptation_for_extinct_life':adapt,'copying_or_accuracy_risk':risk})
 assert seen==list(range(len(cues))), 'Every retrieved cue must be coded exactly once'
 name=case['key'];duration=case['publisher_duration_seconds'];rows=[]
 for sec in range(math.ceil(duration)):
  active=[c for c in cues if c['start']<sec+1 and c['end']>sec];bs=[b for b in beats if b['start']<sec+1 and b['end']>sec]
  rows.append({'case':name,'publisher_second':sec,'active_caption_cues':';'.join(str(c['cue']) for c in active),'caption_terms':';'.join(sorted({t for c in active for t in c['terms']})),'manual_rhetorical_beats':';'.join(str(b['beat']) for b in bs),'beat_roles':';'.join(b['role'] for b in bs),'evidence_state':'caption_window' if active else 'no_active_caption; audio/visual state unknown','visuals_audio_retention':'not reviewed / not available'})
 with (OUT/f'{name}-every-second.csv').open('w',newline='') as f:
  w=csv.DictWriter(f,fieldnames=list(rows[0]));w.writeheader();w.writerows(rows)
 (OUT/f'{name}-coded-beats.json').write_text(json.dumps(beats,indent=2)+'\n')
 text=[f'# {case["title"]}: cue-complete textual forensic coding',f'\nSource: {case["public_url"]}',f'\nEdition: PBS, {duration}s; {len(cues)} timed cues; {case["caption_word_count"]} caption tokens. Not YouTube-edit timing; not a playback/retention study.\n']
 for b in beats:
  text.extend([f'## {b["beat"]:02}. {b["start"]:.3f}–{b["end"]:.3f}s | {b["role"]}',f'Caption cues: {b["first_cue"]}–{b["last_cue"]}.',f'\n**Observed text structure:** {b["observed_textual_mechanism"]}',f'\n**Adaptation, not copying:** {b["adaptation_for_extinct_life"]}',f'\n**Risk / boundary:** {b["copying_or_accuracy_risk"]}',f'\n**Attention claim:** {b["editorial_attention_hypothesis"]}\n'])
 (OUT/f'{name}-microscope.md').write_text('\n'.join(text)+'\n')
 summary.append({'case':name,'source_url':case['public_url'],'publisher_seconds':duration,'caption_end_seconds':case['last_cue_end'],'caption_cues':len(cues),'manually_coded_beats':len(beats),'caption_word_count':case['caption_word_count'],'approx_caption_tokens_per_publisher_minute':round(case['caption_word_count']/duration*60,2),'second_grid_rows':len(rows),'timing_precision':'caption cues, not word onsets or edit cuts','caption_sha256':case['caption_sha256']})
(OUT/'coverage.json').write_text(json.dumps({'cases':summary,'causal_retention_claim':False,'full_third_party_scripts_redistributed':False,'scope':'Two cue-complete PBS-edition textual case studies plus separate untimed/control discussion. Not an exhaustive current-growing-channel study.'},indent=2)+'\n')
print(json.dumps(summary,indent=2))
